# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy this file to .env.local if you want to set anything here. NOTHING in it
# is required: the five effects, the Lab, the chain and the export all run
# without a key.
#
# The AI panel (Gemini 3.8: Art Director, Agent, Optimizer) needs a Gemini API
# key. The normal way is to PASTE it in the Gemini 3.8 panel: the badge turns
# from grey STANDBY to green ACTIVE and the three modes unlock. The pasted key
# stays in your browser and is only ever sent to this app's own server.
#
# A free key, no credit card: https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
#
# The server prints one line on every boot — the model it will use and whether
# it has a key of its own. Read that line.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Optional: a key on the server ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set this only if this machine should work without pasting the key in the
# panel. A key pasted in the panel always takes precedence over this one.
# It is used ONLY for the browser on this same machine (localhost): a phone or
# another laptop on the Wi-Fi that opens http://<this-machine-ip>:3000 must
# paste its own key, so nobody on the network can spend your quota — and so
# must anyone who reaches the app through a reverse proxy or a tunnel.
# It is used ONLY by `npm run dev` and by the desktop app. A BUILT server
# (`npm run build` + `npm start`, i.e. a deploy) IGNORES it, always: behind a
# reverse proxy a visitor can look exactly like a browser on this machine.
# Every user of a deployed server pastes their own key.
GEMINI_API_KEY=""

# ── Optional: the model ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Default: gemini-3.8-flash. Override it only if Google rejects the default for
# your key — the panel then says the model is not available, and the server log
# shows `[gemini] model_unavailable: ...`.
# GEMINI_MODEL="gemini-3.8-flash"

# ── Optional: the port ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Default 3000. Change it only if something else already uses that port.
# PORT=3000

# ── Optional: the bind address ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Default: every network interface (0.0.0.0), so a phone or an iPad on the same
# Wi-Fi can open the dev app at http://<this-machine-ip>:3000. Set it to
# 127.0.0.1 to keep the server on this machine only (it then also hides the dev
# source tree from the LAN). The desktop app always binds 127.0.0.1.
# (Named SYNTECH_HOST, not HOST: some shells and containers export HOST for
# their own purposes, and it must not move the server off localhost.)
# SYNTECH_HOST=127.0.0.1

# ── Optional: behind a reverse proxy (deploy) ───────────────────────────────
# The server limits each client (requests per minute, uploads and Gemini calls
# in flight). By default a client is the address the connection comes from —
# behind nginx / Caddy / a PaaS router that is the PROXY, so every visitor
# would share one budget. Set TRUST_PROXY to how many proxies sit in front of
# this server (1 for a single nginx, 2 for a CDN + nginx, …; "true" means 1):
# the client is then read from X-Forwarded-For, HTTPS from X-Forwarded-Proto
# (which turns on Strict-Transport-Security), and the page's own name from
# X-Forwarded-Host. Set it ONLY when such a proxy really is in front and
# overwrites those headers — otherwise anyone can write them and pick their
# own budget. It never lends out the server key (a built server never does).
# Accepted: a whole number of hops, or true/yes/on (= 1); 0/false/no/off or
# empty = off. Anything else ("-1", "2.5", a typo) is IGNORED (off) with a
# warning in the log — never silently 1.
# Leave it unset for `npm run dev` and the desktop app.
#
# The proxy must also pass the BROWSER's host name on. The AI panel's requests
# carry the page's Origin (https://app.example.com); if the proxy rewrites the
# Host to its upstream (nginx's default proxy_pass sends Host: 127.0.0.1:3000)
# every panel request is refused "403 Cross-site request refused" and the log
# says `[origin] refused /api from a page on "app.example.com"`. Fix it with
#   proxy_set_header Host $host;                 (nginx; Caddy does this already)
# or set TRUST_PROXY and have the proxy send
#   proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host $host;
# (and list that name in ALLOWED_HOSTS below).
#
# What a "client" is, honestly: one IPv4 address, or one IPv6 /64. Everyone
# behind one IPv4 NAT (an office, a café, a mobile carrier) shares ONE budget;
# a home line with an IPv6 /56 owns 256 of them. Real per-user limits need
# accounts.
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# ── Optional: how much the AI may do at once (deploy) ───────────────────────
# Per client the limits are fixed: 1 upload and 2 Gemini calls at a time, 30
# AI requests a minute. These set the totals for the whole server (positive
# whole numbers; anything else falls back to the default with a warning).
# A pasted key is proven with Google (one cheap models.get, cached 10 min)
# before a request may take any of these, so a made-up key holds nothing.
#
# Uploads at once. Each streams the user's whole video to the temp folder
# (500 MB at most) before it goes to Google: worst case 6 x 500 MB = 3 GB of
# temp DISK.
# SYNTECH_MAX_UPLOADS=6
#
# Gemini calls (Art Director / Agent / Optimizer) at once.
# SYNTECH_MAX_ROLE_CALLS=16
#
# Megabytes of Gemini request bodies (frames + a short clip, base64 JSON, at
# most 40 MB each; a body of unknown length counts as 40) in flight at once,
# all calls together. This is what bounds RAM: each MB of body is held about
# three times while it is parsed and sent (raw bytes, text, JSON) — measured:
# four 39 MB calls at once took the server from 75 MB to 0.5–0.6 GB. So the
# default 160 means roughly 0.5 GB of RAM at worst, on top of the app itself.
# The panel's requests are usually a few MB, so 160 still lets the 16 calls
# above run at once. A value under 40 still lets one call through at a time.
# SYNTECH_ROLE_BYTES_MB=160

# ── Optional: other names the app is opened by ───────────────────────────────
# The AI endpoints (/api) answer only when the address bar shows localhost, an
# IP address (http://192.168.1.20:3000 from a phone on the Wi-Fi) or a .local
# name (http://studio-mac.local:3000 from the iPad: the Mac's own Bonjour
# name). Any other name gets "403 — this server's API does not answer on …",
# and the Gemini panel stays locked even with a valid key. That stops a web
# page on some other site from talking to this server through your browser.
# To open the app under another name (a reverse proxy, a tunnel, a cloud
# deploy), list it here, comma-separated, then restart the server:
#   ALLOWED_HOSTS="studio.example.com"            exactly that name
#   ALLOWED_HOSTS=".example.com"                  example.com and every subdomain
#                                                 ("*.example.com" means the same)
#   ALLOWED_HOSTS="*"                             no check at all — only behind a
#                                                 proxy that checks the name itself
# The same list also lets the page itself load under that name in dev.
# A name listed here never lends out the key above: whoever opens the app
# through it pastes their own key in the panel.
# ALLOWED_HOSTS=""

# ── Test only ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Points the Gemini client at a local mock of generativelanguage.googleapis.com
# so the verify suites can run the whole AI flow without a real key or network.
# Never set it in real use. Honoured ONLY when it points at this machine
# (127.0.0.1, localhost, [::1]); any other host is ignored with a warning, so
# a planted setting can never send your key to someone else's server.
# GEMINI_BASE_URL="http://127.0.0.1:4010"

# ── Deployment ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where this app is hosted. AI Studio injects it at runtime; unused locally.
# APP_URL=""
